# ===========================================================================
# INSTANCE CONFIGURATION
# ===========================================================================
#
# This repository is ONE deployment. Clone it per instance; do not try to run
# two instances from one checkout.
#
#   git clone <repo> acme-dc && cd acme-dc
#   $EDITOR config/instance.env          <- you are here
#   ./deploy.sh --preflight              <- checks everything, creates nothing
#   DC_API_KEY=... GEMINI_API_KEY=... ./deploy.sh --bootstrap-secrets
#   ./deploy.sh
#
# NO SECRETS IN THIS FILE, ever. API keys go to Secret Manager via
# --bootstrap-secrets, which reads them from your shell and writes nothing to
# disk.
#
# Every value below is REQUIRED and has no default. That is deliberate: each
# one decides where your data lives, what it costs, or who can reach it, and a
# silent default for any of them is a decision made on your behalf that you
# would not see until the bill or the latency showed up.
# ===========================================================================


# ── 1. Where it runs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Your GCP project id. Billing must be enabled on it.
PROJECT_ID=""

# The region for Cloud Run, Cloud SQL, buckets and Artifact Registry.
# There is deliberately no default. Pick the one closest to your users, and
# check your data-residency obligations before you do — moving afterwards
# means recreating everything.
#   e.g. us-central1 · europe-west1 · asia-south1
REGION=""

# Short name for this deployment. Lowercase, DNS-safe.
# Names every resource and the Terraform state prefix, so two clones pointed at
# one project stay separate: acme-prod and acme-staging never collide.
INSTANCE=""


# ── 2. Which data plane serves it ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   none  Public datacommons.org. Nothing to provision, no database, no bill.
#         Cannot serve your own datasets. Start here.
#   dcp   Google's Data Commons Platform — Spanner, managed ingestion.
#         Provisioned separately by datacommons-cli; fill in the two values
#         below from its `terraform output`.
#   cdc   The self-hosted plane — this repo's services image over Cloud SQL,
#         plus an ingest job. Everything created by this deploy.
DATA_BACKEND=""

# Required when DATA_BACKEND="dcp", ignored otherwise.
#   terraform output datacommons_service_url
#   terraform output datacommons_service_name
# The NAME matters as much as the URL: it is what this deployment's service
# account is granted run.invoker on. Without that grant the backend refuses
# every call, and it shows up as "no data" rather than as an error.
DCP_SERVICE_URL=""
DCP_SERVICE_NAME=""

# Required when DATA_BACKEND="none", ignored otherwise.
#
# TWO hosts, not one, and this is the only backend where they differ. Getting it
# wrong produces a deployment where chat answers correctly and no chart ever
# renders: the browser's requests reach a host that does not serve those routes
# and come back as Cloud Endpoints 404s, which nothing surfaces server-side.
#
#   PUBLIC_DC_URL      the versioned REST API, and /mcp -- what the AGENT calls.
#                      The agent's MCP endpoint is this value with /mcp appended.
#   PUBLIC_DC_WEB_URL  the website routes the chart components call from the
#                      BROWSER: /api/observations/series, /api/place/name,
#                      /core/api/... These exist only on the website host.
#
# Standard values, and what you want unless you are pointing at a different
# public Data Commons deployment:
#   PUBLIC_DC_URL="https://api.datacommons.org"
#   PUBLIC_DC_WEB_URL="https://datacommons.org"
PUBLIC_DC_URL=""
PUBLIC_DC_WEB_URL=""

# Required when DATA_BACKEND="cdc", ignored otherwise.
# These bill by the hour, so they are explicit rather than defaulted.
#   CLOUDSQL_TIER                 db-g1-small is fine for a pilot;
#                                 db-custom-2-7680 for production load.
#   CLOUDSQL_AVAILABILITY_TYPE    REGIONAL is highly available and bills for
#                                 two zones. ZONAL is one zone and cheaper —
#                                 correct for a demo or review stack.
CLOUDSQL_TIER=""
CLOUDSQL_AVAILABILITY_TYPE=""


# ── 3. Who can reach it ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   public   Anyone with the URL. This includes the chat endpoint, so it
#            spends your Gemini quota on every request from anyone.
#            Many organisations forbid this — see the note below.
#   iap      Google sign-in in front of the app. Only AUTHORIZED_MEMBERS get
#            in. Requires one manual step, once per project: create the OAuth
#            consent screen in the console. ./deploy.sh --preflight checks it.
#   private  No public access and no sign-in. Reach it only through
#            `gcloud run services proxy`. Good for a pilot you drive yourself.
#
# If your organisation enforces Domain Restricted Sharing, "public" will be
# refused and you want "iap". --preflight detects this before deploying.
ACCESS_MODE=""

# Comma-separated. Each entry prefixed user: or group:.
# Required for "iap" and "private". Leave empty for "public".
#   e.g. group:data-team@acme.com,user:ana@acme.com
AUTHORIZED_MEMBERS=""


# ── 4. Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Defaults to <PROJECT_ID>-<INSTANCE>-config. Set only to keep an existing
# deployment pointed at a bucket it already uses.
#CONFIG_BUCKET=""
